problems.problem:
Sets $A$ and $B$ are non-empty subsets of the set $\mathbb{Z}$ of all integers such that:
(i) every integer belongs to at least one of the sets $A$ and $B$;
(ii) for every number $x$ in the set $A$, the number $x-1$ is in the set $B$;
(iii) for every pair $(x,y)$ of (not necessarily distinct) numbers of the set $B$, the number $x+y$ is in the set $A$.
Determine:
(a) at least one number which is guaranteed to be in the set $A$;
(b) all possible such pairs of sets $(A,B)$.
